From 7989228df3a93804516f09af691cc29949b98efa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 03:25:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: what the display decides of native Dolby
 Vision, in one header

What CWinSystemGbm asks of the display information for native Dolby Vision output was spread over
SupportsNativeDVOutput(), NativeDVLowLatency(), NativeDVTakesRate(),
DisplayDolbyVisionDmVersion() and DVCapabilities(): which signal native output sends (the Standard
mode, or Low-Latency where the display offers no other and it is turned on), what of the display
keeps it away (the reason codes invalid, legacy, lloff and display), whether the signal needs QS,
whether the display takes it above 30 Hz at 2160p, which display management version the metadata
are for, and what of it counts as another display when its EDID changes.

They are now the functions of DV_DISPLAY (DVDisplaySignal.h, pure: the settings come in as
functions, asked only where the display makes them matter, as before), which those methods call,
so that a host test can ask them of the EDIDs of many displays, and the next commit can add a
signal to them in one place. NativeDVSignal() says which signal; NativeDVLowLatency() stays, as
that signal being Low-Latency.

No change of behaviour: the same reasons with the same texts, in the same order before and after
the questions of the output, the same reads of the settings at the same moments, the same tuple of
what native output depends on. Checked on the host by the test of the next commit, which compares
these decisions with those of the code before this commit on the 603 EDIDs of the corpus and on
mutations of them.
